Third-party material bundled with coreval
=========================================

coreval's own R code is MIT licensed; see the package's LICENSE file.

The files below are NOT coreval's own work. Most come from three
MIT-licensed repositories, two published by CDISC and one by Verisian, and
every notice that licence requires is reproduced in full at the end of this
file. Two more, the W3C's XHTML schema modules and IBM's JSONata, are
described in their own sections below.

  * cdisc-org/cdisc-open-rules      - the SENDIG, TIG and USDM rule
                                      definitions and their test data
  * verisianHQ/core-contributor     - the CORE 1.0 SDTMIG and FDA Business
                                      Rules definitions
  * cdisc-org/cdisc-rules-engine    - the offline standards-metadata caches
                                      that project commits to its own git

Each entry below names which it came from. The exact commit each release was
built from is recorded in data-raw/UPSTREAM_SHA (cdisc-open-rules),
data-raw/UPSTREAM_SHA_CORE1 (core-contributor) and data-raw/UPSTREAM_SHA_ENGINE
(metadata) in the source repository; data-raw is not installed, so both rules
commits are also carried at run time on attr(list_rules(), "rules_version")
and written into every findings file.

Where CORE 1.0's engine, verisianHQ/cdisc-rules-engine, reads a rule
differently from CDISC's, coreval follows it for the 1.0 rules. That engine is
a fork of cdisc-org/cdisc-rules-engine under the same MIT notice reproduced
below. No code from it is bundled; a few small reference tables are (see
core1_reference.rds and sdtm_model_versions.rds), at the commit recorded in
data-raw/UPSTREAM_SHA_CORE1_ENGINE.


inst/extdata/rules.rds
----------------------
From verisianHQ/core-contributor and cdisc-org/cdisc-open-rules. The
conformance rule definitions - the check logic, scope, messages, cited
guidance and conformance-rule identifiers - extracted at build time from a
pinned commit of each. The SDTMIG rules come from core-contributor, where CORE
1.0 moved them; everything else, and CDISC's own version of a rule both carry
for the SEND and TIG studies it names, comes from cdisc-open-rules.

inst/extdata/core1_reference.rds
--------------------------------
From verisianHQ/cdisc-rules-engine. Reference tables CORE 1.0's own rules
read: the FDA-supported Therapeutic Area User Guides and the rows of the FDA
Data Standards Catalog (resources/helper_data/), and the ISO 3166-1 and GENC
country names and codes (sql_operations/get_countries.py). They restate
public FDA, ISO and GENC information as that repository publishes it, under
its MIT notice reproduced below.

